\card{Definition}{algebra}{
\p{
\label{Algebra}
\leanok
\lean{Algebra}
\uses{RingHom,Module}

Let #{R} be a commutative \vocabk{ring}{ca-000U} with identity.
An \newvocab{algebra} #{A} over #{R} is an associative ring with identity,
equipped with a \vocabk{ring homomorphism}{ca-0014}
#{\rfun : R \to_{+*} A} and a \vocabk{scalar multiplication}{ca-000X}
#{\bu : R \to A \to A}, such that for every #{r \in R} and #{x \in A},
##{
\rfun(r) * x = x * \rfun(r),
\qquad
r \bu x = \rfun(r) * x.
}
The ring homomorphism preserves the identity; multiplication in #{A} need not
be commutative.
}

\p{This is the central-map convention of
\citet{Section 22.2, Tag 09JF}{stacks2017stacks}. It is equivalent to the module formulation in
\citet{Definition 1.6, p. 4}{jadczyk2019notes}; see
[the comparison](ca-0018).}
}
